{"authored" [{"question" "q-80503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/9d4a0a08513d817fe841f1e45e5d363d6e64241837dea71f38aef452f86b08a7.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{x}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nRequire $D\\subseteq R$, so the rectangle contains the entire region. Which of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[0,1]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[0,2]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[0,2]\\times [0,1]$" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-80503/review.json" "file_sha256" "49e66a67a360abfbbcab2d2fc503abc56723a243a9d9feaa79449c81bba7f66a" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-80503/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "a2421cd6717dc0f82de7aa6a0a8456b75996c62ef9a82913d4be576802f98a7a" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-80503/verification.json" "basis" 1799 "rationale" "Make the verified containing-region interpretation explicit in the study request. The prompt now requires D subset R and the worked solution checks each rectangle against the actual ellipse or displayed domain. Zero extension on a containing rectangle preserves the integral. Keys and complete answer fields remain unchanged; original source, grades and interpretations remain preserved. Equality for a particular integrand alone is not a general containment criterion, illustrated decisively by q129666 odd-in-x cancellation."} {"question" "q-80503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The displayed region includes points with $x>1$, so rectangle I, $[0,1]\\times[-1,1]$, does not contain all of $D$. It includes points with $y<0$, so rectangle III, $[0,2]\\times[0,1]$, also fails the containment requirement. The entire drawn region lies in rectangle II, $[0,2]\\times[-1,1]$.\n\nThe region lies strictly to the right of the $y$-axis, so $3y^2/x$ is defined throughout $D$. For rectangle II, $f$ equals this integrand on $D$ and zero outside $D$ in $R$; in particular its zero branch applies on $x=0$, which is outside $D$. Hence $\\iint_R f\\,dA=\\iint_D3y^2/x\\,dA$. The correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-80503/review.json" "file_sha256" "49e66a67a360abfbbcab2d2fc503abc56723a243a9d9feaa79449c81bba7f66a" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-80503/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "a2421cd6717dc0f82de7aa6a0a8456b75996c62ef9a82913d4be576802f98a7a" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-80503/verification.json" "basis" 1799 "rationale" "Make the verified containing-region interpretation explicit in the study request. The prompt now requires D subset R and the worked solution checks each rectangle against the actual ellipse or displayed domain. Zero extension on a containing rectangle preserves the integral. Keys and complete answer fields remain unchanged; original source, grades and interpretations remain preserved. Equality for a particular integrand alone is not a general containment criterion, illustrated decisively by q129666 odd-in-x cancellation."} {"question" "q-80503" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-80503/review.json" "file_sha256" "49e66a67a360abfbbcab2d